{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_12","type":"agent_message","text":"Completed-GMT: 2026-10-05 23:14:58 GMT\nCompleted-Local: 2026-10-06 06:14:58 +0700\n\n**NOT READY — close the grant bypass, restore issue ordering, and complete the required acceptance coverage.**\n\n1. **P1 — Public options can bypass grant and protection checks.** At [handoff.cljc:1823](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-d10/src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/handoff.cljc:1823) and [line 1833](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-d10/src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/handoff.cljc:1833), `:yin.vm.ucf.handoff/install-child` suppresses both custody inspection and `accept-grant!`. Supplying that option to public `resume-task` can restore a blocked version-1 body with `:running` gate and no grant or custody map. Namespacing does not make an option internal. Move recursive child restoration behind a private helper whose child context is established only after root validation and grant acceptance. Add a public-entry regression proving the option cannot bypass authorization and that rejection performs zero attachments.\n\n2. **P1 — Restored puts lack the required issue-order stamps.** [handoff.cljc:1899](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-d10/src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/handoff.cljc:1899) restores `:op-id` but never assigns `:yin.k/issue` or initializes `:yin.k/issued`. The [close ruling explicitly assigns this to D10](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1791198000000-architect-d5-close-ruling.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md:99). Without it, a later close cannot reliably be ordered after restored pending writes. Stamp restored puts in wait order for each machine, children included, and initialize the next issue counter beyond those stamps. Also clear receiver-local `:yin.k/closes` and rebuild `:yin.k/issued`: [isolated-receiver:1775](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-d10/src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/handoff.cljc:1775) currently retains both. Pin restored-put → newly queued close → newly issued put ordering and receiver-state contamination.\n\n3. **P1 — Three refusal assertions do not satisfy four-kernel restoration acceptance.** [handoff_v1_test.cljc:602](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-d10/test/yin/vm/ucf/handoff_v1_test.cljc:602) requires `:unaddressed-segment` for walker, stack and register instead of exercising their lowers. The report correctly discloses this, but disclosure does not close the governing acceptance requirement. Supply valid addressed fixtures for those kernels and test successful restoration; if the existing exporter prevents producing them, resolve that prerequisite or obtain an explicit scope amendment before declaring D10 complete. Keep the refusal regression separately.\n\n4. **P2 — Grant tests do not yet distinguish all required checks.** The implementation checks exact epoch kind and individual prefix sequence numbers, but [the consistency test:388](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-d10/test/yin/vm/ucf/handoff_v1_test.cljc:388) tests a negative epoch and a frontier/count mismatch only. Add an integral float epoch and out-of-range epoch, plus equal-length prefixes with duplicate, skipped or out-of-order sequence numbers. Use the existing refusal helper to assert no `:vm` and zero attachments. These cases must fail even when the other grant fields agree.\n\nThe ordinary root path otherwise implements the seven consistency checks before attachment, recursive protection checks, exact counter restoration, carried IDs, and the specified custody shape. Halted roots correctly restore ended without custody; version-0 branches and existing D9 halted-restoration assertions remain intact. Deferring actual next-ID assignment to D11 and authenticated grant acquisition, admission proof, and live-tenure rechecking to D13 is correct. The reported JVM/Node/Dart results support the exercised paths, not the missing kernel coverage. Static review found no additional portability or formatting blocker. No files were edited and no suites were run."}}
